      "id": 122,
      "text": "your keys, not your coins. Exactly. Because in this system, you are your own bank. Yeah, because your money is secured"
    },
    {
      "id": 123,
      "text": "by a private key, which acts like an incredibly long, mathematically unguessable pin. Right. And if you"
    },
    {
      "id": 124,
      "text": "use a non-custodial wallet, like a hardware device or a proper app on your phone, you hold that pin. But if you use"
    },
    {
      "id": 125,
      "text": "a custodial service, like leaving your funds sitting on a major web exchange,"
    },
    {
      "id": 126,
      "text": "then they hold the pin. You don't actually have cryptocurrency; you just have an IOU from a company promising to give you"
    },
    {
      "id": 127,
      "text": "the money when you ask for it. And we saw how dangerous centralization was in the 1980s. Exactly. When you hold your own keys, you have"
    },
    {
      "id": 128,
      "text": "total financial sovereignty, but I mean, it comes with a massive responsibility. Right. Because if you are your own bank, you are"
    },
    {
      "id": 129,
      "text": "entirely responsible for your own security. Yes. If you mess up, there is no 1-800 customer service number to call to reverse"
    },
    {
      "id": 130,
      "text": "a transaction. Which brings us to the seed phrase or recovery code. Right. When you set up a true non-custodial wallet,"
    },
    {
      "id": 131,
      "text": "it generates a list of 12 or 24 random English words. Yep. The seed phrase. It gives you words"
    },
    {
      "id": 132,
      "text": "like media, suspect, effort, dish, album. To a beginner, it just sounds like absolute nonsense. Totally"
    },
    {
      "id": 133,
      "text": "random. But those words are the master backup of your entire financial life on that wallet. It's crucial to understand the"
    },
    {
      "id": 134,
      "text": "cryptography behind this, though. Those 12 words aren't just a fancy password you create. What are they? They"
    },
    {
      "id": 135,
      "text": "are pulled from a standardized list of 2048 words. The specific sequence of those words is actually"
    },
    {
      "id": 136,
      "text": "a mathematical representation of a massive 128-bit or 256-bit integer. Oh,"
    },
    {
      "id": 137,
      "text": "wow. Yeah. That string of words is your master private key. Just translate it into a human-"
    },
    {
      "id": 138,
      "text": "readable format. So the algorithm uses those words to mathematically derive every single private key and public"
    },
    {
      "id": 139,
      "text": "address your wallet will ever use. Exactly. Which means if you drop your phone in a lake, your money isn't just gone. Nope. You just buy a"
    }
  ]
